[Music]  this morning on the dish australian-born  chef Jeanine booth born into a family of  restaurant owners she was inspired by  the food of Australia's many cultures  and decided to become a chef after  starting her career in Miami she took  her love of Southern cuisine north to  New York in 2014 she and partner Jeff  McInnis opened Rutten Bone celebrating  southern fare and country comforts they  followed up with a second branch in  Miami Beach and last year added stilts  ville fish bar offering local locally  sourced seafood in Miami's sunset Harbor  neighborhood chef Jeanine booth good  morning welcome to the dish why are you  thank you so much for having me thanks  for coming we are overwhelmed by the  size of this chicken look of that cake  tell us what else is in store for us  here so we have a little buffet here  today kind of a journey through all of  the restaurants that I've opened over  the last few years so we have this  coconut shrimp which is a dish that we  have on our restaurant in Miami called  the silk film it's a coconut shrimp  these are our deviled eggs from Rudin  born here in New York City and we also  have a location in Miami and this is the  fried chicken from written one it's a  signature dish without buttermilk  biscuits and there's a little lemon  powder on the fried chicken it gives the  chicken I'm a really nice ding and then  just some simple root vegetables and of  course we've got to finish with dessert  right so this is a lemon cake decadent  with some whipped cream and strawberries  very simple stuff but really really nice  elevated versions of all the classic  dishes that you're really really  accustomed to please tell me what this  so I love gin I love champagne I love  strawberries and it's summer right now  so rhubarb is insane so we made a little  spritz that for you guys the next  refresh is delicious so you grew up in  the restaurant industry as I said but  when did you know that food was your  passion and was what you were going to  do so I was probably around 12 years old  I started taking cooking class lessons  at school and my parents loved to cook  but really my dad actually owned a  couple of restaurants when I was growing  up and so I kind of grew up in the  industry but he stay away from it he did  yeah he was  look you can you can go into the  industry if you want but you know you're  gonna be working every weekend and a lot  of holidays and and you know you're not  gonna get to spend a lot of time with  your family okay and I tried to you know  go into different fields and I went to  tried nursing I tried teaching I know  and I and my parents like what is going  on she's going in all these different  directions and eventually I ended up  back in culinary school and that was  when I really just found my place and  and it was just it just really made  sense for me but you had a stint of  traveling the world as well I think  there's nothing better than backpacking  through countries so yeah I left high  school and I saved up all of my money I  I worked in like an administration job  and I just saved up all my money and I  was really focused I wanted to go  traveling and really save the world and  and discover more about different  coaches and cuisines and so it was a  really great time I traveled for about  nine months and all through Europe and  Thailand and Indonesia and it was just a  really eye-opening experience of me and  it was really really special and then  when I got home to Perth my parents are  like okay it's time for me it was over a  meal in Miami that you met Jeff McInnis  right that's really what started this  partnership yes so I met Jeff at a  restaurant called GG's he was working at  he was the chef and I ate this meatloaf  dish and it was absolutely incredible  hmm and I had just moved from Australia  and I never really had meatloaf before  and Mike is if this is what meatloaf is  then like I know exactly what everyone's  been talking about this whole time but  it was like great short rib and it was  it was not your regular meatloaf and so  I asked to meet the chef because I just  wanted to figure out how to make the  dish and he was too busy and I actually  went back on the same night and ate the  same dish a second time that night  because it was just so good and it was  all I could think about and eventually  he was like oh is that go back again I  better go made pressure  okay so he was like well if you want to  learn how to make this meatloaf I'm not  just gonna give out my recipe you have  to come work for me so I was in culinary  school at the time so it was a perfect  kind of introduction to the the industry  so as I hand you this dish to sign yeah  if you could have this meal with anyone  past or present who would it be  so obviously I'm from Australia and all  my family is still out there and I get  back about once a year  but I would definitely want to share it  with them with my parents and my sisters  and my nephews and my daughter Sonny and  Brice and and Jeff of course awesome  well we thank you chef Jeanine booth we  so are excited about digging in aren't  we and for more of chef Boots's booth  and the dish head to our website at CBS  this morning calm

